# Feature-Sliced Design

## Layers

Imports only ever point downwards in this list.

| Layer      | Holds                                               | Example                       |
| ---------- | --------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |
| `app`      | Bootstrap: providers, router, the single stylesheet | `src/app/providers`           |
| `pages`    | One screen, composing widgets and features          | `src/pages/product-detail`    |
| `widgets`  | A self-contained block combining several features   | `src/widgets/product-list`    |
| `features` | One user action                                     | `src/features/create-product` |
| `entities` | A business object: its type, API and presentation   | `src/entities/product`        |
| `shared`   | Everything domain-agnostic                          | `src/shared/lib`              |

`shared` is not sliced; it is split into segments (`api`, `config`, `lib`, `ui`, `i18n`, `seed`) and
any segment may be imported directly. Every other layer is split into slices, and a slice is only
reachable through its `index.ts`.

```ts
import { ProductCard } from "@/entities/product";              // correct
import { ProductCard } from "@/entities/product/ui/product-card"; // ESLint error
```

## Inside a slice

```
src/features/create-product/
├── index.ts          public API — the only file others may import
├── model/            state, hooks, validation
├── ui/               components
└── api/              requests specific to this slice
```

`index.ts` re-exports named symbols explicitly. No `export *`.

## Adding a new feature

1. Decide the layer by asking what the code is _about_. A business object is an entity; a thing the
   user _does_ is a feature; a screen is a page.
2. Create `src/<layer>/<slice>/` with `ui/`, `model/` as needed and an `index.ts`.
3. Add the route in `src/app/routes/` if it needs a URL. Route files stay thin: they import a page
   component and nothing else.
4. Put translations in `src/shared/i18n/locales/{ko,en}/`.
5. Run `pnpm verify`.

## Routing

Routes are file-based, generated into `src/app/routeTree.gen.ts` by `pnpm routes:gen` (which
`pnpm build` and `pnpm typecheck` run for you). Never edit the generated file.

Data loading belongs to TanStack Query. A route may prefetch with
`queryClient.ensureQueryData(...)` in its `loader`; the component still calls `useQuery` so it stays
usable outside that route.

Query keys live in `src/shared/api/query-keys.ts` so invalidation stays predictable.

## State

- Server data → TanStack Query. Never mirror it into Zustand.
- Shared client state → a Zustand store in the owning slice's `model/`.
- Anything local → `useState`.
